279 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/ActualText (Indian ratsnakes \(Ptyas mucosa\) are able to survive in a wide variety of habitats, and can utilise different resources. In May 2021, the authors found two eggs inside the shell of a deceased Indian black turtle \(Melanochelys trijuga\) in a concrete pit at the University of Goa. Upon careful study, the eggs were identified as those of the Indian ratsnake, and  found to be devoid of embryos. This article is a first such record in Asia of a snake depositing eggs in the shell of a dead turtle. )
/Pg 7 0 R
/K [8]
>>
